course: language
lesson: 40
title: The big language quiz
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: This is the last lesson of the language course: a big quiz that visits everything, from word classes and plurals to British and American words, Vikings, Mandarin tones and Ukrainian cases.
explain: Think of it as a family quiz night: Grandad shouts the questions from the kitchen, everybody grabs a pencil, and every answer can be checked, letter by letter or rule by rule.
explain: Along the way you have seen that every language and every dialect is a full, working system: "arvo" in Sydney, "craic" in Dublin, "cwtch" in Cardiff, "Мамо!" in Kyiv and "mā" in Beijing all do their job perfectly.
explain: Languages keep changing, borrowing and playing, so the best language skill is curiosity: notice odd words, ask what they mean and enjoy how people talk.
fact: An adverb describes a verb, often telling how something happens: in "She ran quickly", "quickly" is an adverb.
fact: Some plurals don't add -s: goose becomes geese, mouse becomes mice, child becomes children, and sheep and moose stay the same.
fact: "Its" with no apostrophe means belonging to it ("The dog wagged its tail"); "it's" means it is or it has.
fact: Some verbs have irregular past tenses: bring becomes brought, catch becomes caught, and swim becomes swam.
fact: British biscuit, pavement and lift are American cookie, sidewalk and elevator.
fact: "They", "sky" and "egg" came from Old Norse, the language of the Vikings, who settled in England from about 800 AD.
fact: "Arvo" means afternoon in Australia, "craic" means fun or a good time in Ireland, and "cwtch" means a hug or cuddle in Wales.
fact: A comma can save Grandma: "Let's eat, Grandma" talks to Grandma; "Let's eat Grandma" sounds like she is dinner.
fact: In Mandarin, "mā" (high, flat tone) means mother and "mǎ" (dipping tone) means horse; the tone makes them different words.
fact: Ukrainian has seven cases; the vocative is the case for calling someone, so you call your mum "Мамо!".
fact: "Emoji" is Japanese: "e" means picture and "moji" means character; the likeness to "emotion" is a coincidence.
exercise: What kind of word is "quickly" in "She ran quickly"? | An adverb | An adjective | A noun | A verb
exercise: What is the plural of "goose"? | Geese | Gooses | Goose | Geeses
exercise: What is the plural of "mouse" (the animal)? | Mice | Mouses | Meese | Mousen
exercise: What are the plurals of "sheep" and "moose"? | Sheep and moose | Sheeps and mooses | Sheep and meese | Sheeps and moose
exercise: What is the plural of "child"? | Children | Childs | Childer | Childes
exercise: Which is right? | The dog wagged its tail | The dog wagged it's tail | The dog wagged its' tail | The dog wagged it is tail
exercise: What is the past tense of "bring"? | Brought | Bringed | Brang | Brung
exercise: What are the past tenses of "catch" and "swim"? | Caught and swam | Catched and swimmed | Caught and swimmed | Catched and swum
exercise: British "biscuit", "pavement" and "lift" are what in American English? | Cookie, sidewalk, elevator | Cracker, road, escalator | Cookie, street, stairway | Muffin, sidewalk, hoist
exercise: Which language gave English "they", "sky" and "egg"? | Old Norse | Old French | Latin | Old German
exercise: What does "arvo" mean in Australia? | Afternoon | Avocado | Morning | A party
exercise: What does "craic" mean in Ireland? | Fun, a good time | A crack in a wall | A small crab | A cold drink
exercise: What does "cwtch" mean in Wales? | A hug or a cuddle | A small kitchen | A rainy day | A cheeky child
exercise: Add a comma to save Grandma: "Let's eat Grandma." | Let's eat, Grandma. | Let's, eat Grandma. | Let's eat Grandma, | Let's eat Gran, ma.
exercise: In Mandarin, "mā" and "mǎ" have the same letters. What makes them different words? | The tone | The spelling | The speed | The volume
exercise: How many cases does Ukrainian have? | Seven | Three | Two | Twelve
exercise: Which case do you use to call your mum in Ukrainian, as in "Мамо!"? | The vocative | The plural | The future | The feminine
exercise: What does "emoji" mean in Japanese? | Picture character | Emotion icon | Smiley face | Little message
test: Your mate goes "quickly's an adjective, it describes stuff, innit." What's true? | It describes how she ran, so it's an adverb | He's right, every describing word is an adjective | It's a noun, because it names a speed | It's a verb, because running is an action
test: Your little cousin says "I saw three gooses and two mouses." What's the right way to say it? | Three geese and two mice | Three geeses and two mices | Three goose and two mouse | Three gooses and two mice
test: Your sis texts "the cat licked it's paw lol." What should she fix? | Its, with no apostrophe, means belonging to it | Nothing, "it's" always means belonging to it | It should be "its'" with the apostrophe at the end | It should be "it is paw", written out in full
test: Your grandad from Yorkshire says "ah brung thee a present, lad." ("Thee" is an old word for you.) In standard English what is the past of "bring"? | Brought | Bringed | Brang | Brung
test: Your Aussie mate texts "footy this arvo? then a cwtch with the dog lol." ("Footy" is football; "cwtch" is a Welsh word he picked up.) When is the football, and what's after? | This afternoon, then a cuddle with the dog | This morning, then a walk with the dog | Tonight, then a bath for the dog | Tomorrow, then dinner with the dog
test: Your mate from Dublin says "the craic was mighty last night." What does she mean? | It was great fun | A loud crack | It was cold | A big fight
test: A Gen Alpha kid says "emoji means emotion icon, no cap." ("No cap" means honestly.) What's the real answer? | It's Japanese for picture character | He's right, it's English for emotion icon | It's French for little face | It's Latin for feeling
test: What would happen if you wrote "Let's eat Grandma!" on Grandma's birthday card with no comma? | It would sound like you plan to eat her | Nothing, commas never change meaning | It would mean Grandma is cooking | It would turn into a question
test: Your bro says "the Vikings just smashed stuff and left no words, bruv." Which everyday words prove him wrong? | They, sky and egg | Beef, pork and chef | Video, audio and bus | Pyjamas, jungle and loot
test: You call "Мама!" across a Ukrainian playground, and your friend smiles and says the calling form. What is it? | Мамо! | Мами! | Маму! | Мамою!
test: You say "mǎ" to your Mandarin teacher when you meant mother. What did you really say? | Horse | Mother | Hemp | Scold
test: Your American pen pal asks for a "cookie, the sidewalk and the elevator" in British English. What are they? | A biscuit, the pavement and the lift | A scone, the road and the stairs | A cracker, the kerb and the escalator | A muffin, the path and the hoist
exam: Ascertain the grammatical category of "quickly" in the sentence "She ran quickly". | Adverb | Adjective | Noun | Preposition
exam: Identify the correctly punctuated sentence, consistent with the established conventions of English. | The dog wagged its tail. | The dog wagged it's tail. | The dog wagged its' tail. | The dog wagged it's' tail.
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the distinction between the Mandarin syllables "mā" and "mǎ"? | A difference of tone, signifying mother and horse respectively | A difference of spelling, signifying horse and mother respectively | A difference of volume alone, without any change of meaning | A difference of regional accent, both signifying mother
exam: State the number of grammatical cases possessed by Ukrainian, and the case employed when addressing one's mother. | Seven; the vocative | Three; the nominative | Two; the genitive | Twelve; the accusative
